# The unpinned list carried this message as unreachable — "the lexer folds an
# indented line into its head unless that head opens one" — but `two = if`
# opens a block, the lexer keeps the branch lines, and the checker refuses
# the shape with these words. Whether the refusal or its wording is the
# right one for a block-if constant is a filed question; this pins what the
# compiler says today so any change is deliberate.
two = if (1 < 2)
  "a"
else
  "b"

pub play = print "{two}"
